<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulrich Wille</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulrich Wille was a Swiss army officer who served as commander-in-chief, with the rank of general, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, from 1914 to 1918. He sought to modernise the militia army along Prussian lines of discipline and training. His open sympathy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> caused controversy in French-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and he deployed troops against the general strike of November 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulrich-wille.jpg" alt="Ulrich Wille" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Wille_-_CH-BAR_-_3237761_cropped.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Conrad Ulrich Sigmund Wille was born on 5 April 1848 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, the son of the journalist François Wille and the novelist Eliza Wille. The family came from La Sagne in the French-speaking canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a>; an ancestor, Henri Vuille, had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and altered the name. François Wille sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt National Assembly</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a>–1849, and after their failure the family settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1851, at the estate of Mariafeld in Meilen in the canton of Zürich.</p><p>Wille studied law at Zürich, Halle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, where he took a doctorate in 1869. In 1872 he married Constanza Maria Amalia Clara von Bismarck, a daughter of Lieutenant-General Friedrich Wilhelm von Bismarck. Their eldest son, also named Ulrich Wille, became a corps commander whose pro-German views later added to his tensions with General Henri Guisan, and their granddaughter was the author and photographer Annemarie Schwarzenbach.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a lieutenant Wille served on the borders during the mobilisation of 1870–1871, and in 1871 he began a career as an artillery instructor, training with the 1st Artillery Regiment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s Guard Corps and at the United Artillery and Engineering School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. He completed the Federal Instructor School in 1872, became a captain on the federal artillery staff two years later, a colonel in 1885 and commander of the cavalry in 1892.</p><p>On 3 August 1914 the United Federal Assembly elected him general with 122 votes against 63 for Theophil Sprecher von Bernegg, who became his chief of the general staff. Wille concentrated 238,000 men and 50,000 horses near the borders, especially in Ajoie and Engadin. His proposal to the Federal Council on 20 July 1915 to enter the war on the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> caused a scandal in French-speaking Switzerland, as did the 20 days' detention he gave the two colonels of the Colonels' Affair of 1916.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>From 1872, as an instructor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thun">Thun</a>, Wille wrote articles on military policy, and in 1880 he bought the Zeitschrift für Artillerie und Genie, which he used to campaign for training on the Prussian model. In a note of 10 November 1918, on the eve of the general strike, he wrote that the congresses of Zimmerwald and Kiental had planned to begin the overthrow of the established order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, while urging that the Confederation intervene in time to prevent civil war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulrich Wille” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In both world wars the army mobilised under an elected general, <b>Ulrich Wille</b> in 1914 and Henri Guisan in 1939; in 1940 Guisan announced the strategy of the National Redoubt, a defence of the Alpine core.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Meilen, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.55,10&amp;year=1898" aria-label="Hamburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4772 323 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ulrich Wille"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5222 593h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5222 593h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.55,10&amp;year=1898">What was here in 1898? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
